---
title: "The multi-page subsystem"
---

A directory with a `_site.yml` is a **project**: a website or a book. The core's
`site/` module owns everything the
single-page path never had, the project-level concerns, while the per-page server
that drives it lives in [the dev server](server.tmd). This chapter is the core
subsystem: discovery, the config model, the front-matter primitives, link
rewriting, cross-page references, and search.

```{mermaid}
%%| label: fig-site-render
%%| fig-cap: "A directory is discovered once; each page then runs through the shared core pipeline plus the site-only steps."
flowchart TB
  dir[("directory:<br/>_site.yml + .tmd pages")] --> disc["Site::discover<br/>config · pages · order · xref registry · search index"]
  disc --> page["per page · render_page"]
  page --> core["core pipeline<br/>(.tmd → blocks → HTML)"]
  core --> fm["expand front-matter blocks<br/>about: / hero: / listing:"]
  fm --> chrome["wrap in chrome<br/>navbar / footer / sidebar / prev-next"]
  chrome --> links["rewrite .tmd→.html<br/>+ resolve cross-page xrefs"]
  links --> out["page HTML"]
```

## Discovery

Drop `post.tmd` into a `posts/` folder beside a `_site.yml` and it joins a
website. `Site::discover` reads the root `_site.yml`, then walks the directory for
input pages: every `.tmd` becomes a page, **skipping `_`-prefixed directories**
(partials, `_freeze`, `_site`) and dotfiles. Each page maps to its output URL (`x.tmd` →
`x.html`, mirroring the input tree). Both `build` (static) and `preview` (live) drive a
project through the same `discover` + `render_page`.

Two kinds of `.tmd` are discovered but deliberately kept out of the navigable page set: a
`draft: true` page is excluded from a **website** entirely (no output, nav, listing, or
prev/next entry; books do not consult `draft`), and a deck pulled in by
`{{< embed >}}` is built and served but stays out of nav and search, since it is an
inclusion, not a page. A loose `format: deck` file that no page embeds warns rather
than silently becoming a chrome-wrapped article.

### How `draft:` filtering works

The exclusion is structural, not cosmetic: `website_pages` reads each candidate's front
matter and, on `draft: true`, returns `None` *before* the file ever becomes a `Page`. The
draft therefore never enters `self.pages`, the one list every downstream feature reads
from. Because listings, prev/next navigation, the navbar, the search index, and the
xref registry all derive from `self.pages`, a draft drops out of every one of them at
once, and `build` emits no file for it. (Books are different: a book's page set comes from
the explicit `chapters:` list, so it never walks the directory and never consults
`draft:`; to hold a chapter back from a book, leave it out of `chapters:`.)

### How embedded decks are discovered {#sec-deck-discovery}

A website discovers *every* `.tmd` as a page, so a slide deck that exists only to be
embedded would otherwise become a navigable, chrome-wrapped article with no slides. The
flow that prevents that, @fig-deck-discovery:

```{mermaid}
%%| label: fig-deck-discovery
%%| fig-cap: "Embedded decks are discovered from the pages, then removed from the page set and kept as standalone DeckRefs."
flowchart TB
  pages["pages (every .tmd)"] --> scan["discover_decks:<br/>resolve includes · scan {{< embed >}} targets"]
  scan --> exist{"target is a file?"}
  exist -->|yes| ref["DeckRef { input, url }<br/>(deduped by URL)"]
  exist -->|no| warn["warn: embedded deck not found"]
  ref --> retain["pages.retain(not a deck)"]
  retain --> served["served standalone<br/>(no nav · no search)"]
```

`discover_decks` walks the page set, resolves `{{< include >}}` in each page first (so an
`{{< embed >}}` living inside an included partial is still found), then collects every
embed target. Each target is written relative to its embedding page, so it is mapped back
to a site-root-relative path; a target that is not a real file warns and is skipped (the
embed iframe would otherwise 404). The surviving targets become deduplicated `DeckRef`s,
and `pages.retain(...)` then drops every page whose URL matches a `DeckRef`, so the deck
leaves the navigable set while staying reachable as a standalone deck (the preview looks
it up by output URL via `Site::deck`). What is left after that retain is a true page; a
`format: deck` file *still* in the set is the loose-deck case above, and it warns.

## The config model

The typed `SiteConfig` is Taliesin's **native, flat** schema (title, nav, footer,
`mounts:`, ...). Config is deliberately **two levels with no cascade**: the root
`_site.yml` sets project-wide defaults and a page's own front matter overrides
them, full stop. There is no `_metadata.yml` directory merge, the single most
confusing part of Quarto's model.

The schema is a **closed set**: an unknown top-level key warns with a "did you
mean" hint (`config/mod.rs` `validate_keys`). A Quarto-shaped nested config
(`project:` / `website:` / `book:` / `format:`) is no longer accepted: those keys
warn as unknown and their nested values are ignored, so a Quarto config must be
flattened to the native shape.

`validate_keys` is deliberately a **warning, not an error**: every top-level key in
`_site.yml` is checked against the `NATIVE_KEYS` constant (`title`, `author`,
`description`, `url`, `favicon`, `image`, `output`, `toc`, `css`, `head`, `body-start`,
`body-end`, `nav`, `footer`, `chapters`, `mounts`), and an unknown one is reported but the
rest of the config still parses. The "did you mean" comes from the same `closest()` the
front-matter linter and the CLI's unknown-command suggestion use: the nearest known key
within **Levenshtein edit distance 2** (so `favicn` suggests `favicon`, but a key two-plus
edits away suggests nothing rather than guessing wildly). Two harder failures also degrade
gracefully rather than aborting: a missing `_site.yml` and a YAML file that does not parse
both warn and fall back to a `SiteConfig::default()`, so `check` and `preview` still run
and surface the problem instead of crashing.

::: {.callout-note}
The closed set is the *whole* contract for `_site.yml`: there is no second tier that
tolerates Quarto keys. This is what lets `taliesin schema` generate a JSON Schema directly
from `NATIVE_KEYS`, the very same list `validate_keys` checks against, so the editor
autocomplete and the runtime validator can never drift apart.
:::

Taliesin generates a JSON Schema for `_site.yml` from `NATIVE_KEYS` (the same
closed set the config validator uses), emitted by `taliesin schema`. See the
Configuration reference (`../guide/reference/configuration.html`) for the editor
autocomplete on-ramp.

## Books vs websites

The same machinery produces both, branched on one key: **`chapters:` present ⇒ a
book** (one centred reading column; the chapter list lives in an off-canvas drawer
summoned from a slim sticky topbar, with numbered chapters + sections and
prev/next-*chapter* navigation), otherwise a website (a top navbar). `book.rs` computes
the section numbers that the chapter list and cross-references display. No `type:` is
needed; the shape of the config decides. A `chapters:` entry is a bare path, a
`{ part:, chapters: }` group, or a `{ file:, text: }` mapping whose `text:`
overrides the chapter label (otherwise the label is the chapter's first `# H1`).

## Front-matter primitives

Three front-matter blocks turn a page into something structured, and all three
render into the **framework's own primitives with no bespoke HTML**, the same
primitives the marketing site is built from:

- **`about:`** replaces the title block with a centered profile header (image, name,
  links).
- **`hero:`** replaces it with the `.hero` band (eyebrow, headline, lead, CTA
  buttons). `about:` and `hero:` are mutually exclusive, both stand in for the
  title.
- **`listing:`** crawls a `contents:` directory and emits a card per document (image,
  date, title, description, categories), sorted by date and capped, as a grid or a
  stacked list, so a blog index or a projects page generates from front matter.

## Cross-page links and references

Two related rewrites stitch the pages together:

- **Link rewriting.** Intra-site `.tmd` links (including `other.tmd#anchor`) are
  rewritten to their built `.html` targets, so the source links the way you'd type
  it and the output links correctly.
- **Cross-page cross-references** (`xref.rs`). A project-wide registry scans every
  page for `{#sec-}` / `{#fig-}` / … anchors (plus a section number for numbered book
  sections), then rewrites `data-qmd-xref`-marked links to point at the right page.
  So a `@fig-` in one chapter resolves, with its number, to its figure in another.

The cross-page resolution is a deliberate **two pass** design, because a `@fig-`
reference can point at a label defined on a page that has not been rendered yet:

1. **Scan all pages first.** `scan_xref_targets` walks every page's *source* (a
   lightweight pass, no render, no execution) and records each cross-referenceable anchor
   into a `HashMap<label, XrefTarget>`. The recognized prefixes are `sec-`, `fig-`,
   `tbl-`, `eq-`, `lst-`, `thm-`, and `def-`. Each `XrefTarget` stores the anchor's page
   URL and, for a numbered book section, its section number. Includes are resolved during
   this scan exactly as the render pipeline resolves them, so section counters advance
   over included headings and the numbers match the rendered page. **First definition
   wins** project-wide; if a *different* page redefines the same label, it warns once
   (a within-page duplicate is caught separately by the per-page render).

2. **Rewrite during page render.** When a page is rendered, `cite::process` has already
   turned each `@sec-` reference into a bare-label link that carries a `data-qmd-xref`
   marker naming the anchor:

   ```html
   <a href="#sec-intro" data-qmd-xref="sec-intro">Section</a>
   ```

   `rewrite_cross_refs` then scans that HTML for those markers: a marker whose anchor
   resolves to a *different* page is rewritten to `href="<up><page>#anchor"` with its
   number folded in (and a chapter-level `@sec-` reference reads "Chapter N" the way Quarto
   does), while a same-page or unknown anchor is left exactly as `cite` emitted it. So the
   local case and the cross-page case share one syntax and the reader never sees the seam.

## Full-text search

`search.rs` builds **one cross-page index**: every page's title and each anchored
heading, each carrying the **plain-text body of its section**, so Cmd-K matches
prose, not just headings. It is built once at discovery and **written to a separate
`search.json`, lazy-loaded by the client on first open**, rather than inlined into
every page, so the index never bloats page weight. (On a 17-page book that saved
over a megabyte across the pages.) The palette is opened by the navbar search
control (or the book sidebar's, for a book) or the <kbd>Cmd/Ctrl-K</kbd> shortcut.

`build_index_json` renders each page's Markdown once (no code execution, so the anchor
ids match the served pages exactly), then emits a flat JSON array of compact records, one
per searchable target. Each record is `{u, p, i, l, t, b}`: the page **u**rl, the **p**age
title, the anchor **i**d, the heading **l**evel, the heading **t**ext, and the **b**ody
text of that section. Every page contributes one record for the page itself (id `""`,
level `0`, body = the intro before the first heading) plus one record per anchored heading
(body = the plain text from that heading's close to the next heading's open). The section
body is capped at 1500 characters so one long section or a big code listing cannot blow up
the index, and the author's own `404.html` is skipped so a search never surfaces it.

Search is entirely **in-browser**: there is no server query endpoint. The client fetches
`search.json` once (via `window.QMD_SEARCH_URL`, set in the page head) the first time the
palette opens, caches the parsed array on `window.QMD_SEARCH_INDEX`, and filters it
locally as you type. A static `build` ships the same `search.json` beside the pages, so
search works on a deployed, server-less site identically to the live preview.

::: {.callout-note}
The index is built from `self.pages`, so the two exclusions compound automatically: a
`draft:` page and an embedded deck are both absent from `self.pages`, and therefore absent
from search, without search needing any special case for either.
:::

## Social & SEO metadata

Each page emits OpenGraph, Twitter-card, and `canonical` tags (`meta.rs`), taken from the
page's own front matter (`title`, `description`, `image`) and falling back to the
`_site.yml` config. `og:type` is `article` when the page carries a `date:`, and `website`
otherwise. The absolute-URL tags (`og:url`, `og:image`, `canonical`) are emitted **only
when a `url:` is configured**, since social scrapers need absolute URLs; without a `url:`
the page still carries its relative title/description metadata.

## Mounts

`mounts:` serves *another* project under a URL prefix in `preview`, which is how the
marketing site resolves its `/docs/guide` and `/docs/internals` links live. Under
`preview` a mounted page is **rendered on request as static output**, so it has no live
websocket, no dev menu, and no click-to-source; to edit a mounted book with the full live
loop, preview it directly (`preview docs/internals`) rather than through the mount.

The static `build` does **not** auto-wire mounts: building the site emits each page and its
own assets, but a `mounts:` sub-project is a separate project, so `build <site>` only
*warns* (naming each mount and the exact command) rather than producing it. Build each mount
yourself into the matching sub-path, e.g. `taliesin build docs/internals --out _site/docs/internals`,
so the deployed `/docs/internals/` links resolve. (Auto-building mounts is a planned
follow-up.)

The reason `build` stops at a warning rather than recursing is that a mounted project is a
**genuinely separate project with its own build**: it has its own `_site.yml`, its own
output mode (a book builds to `_book`, a website to `_site`), its own theme and execution
needs, possibly its own kernel. Recursing would mean one `build` silently inheriting and
overriding another project's configuration, the exact directory-cascade ambiguity the
flat, no-cascade config model was designed to avoid. So `build` keeps each project's build
explicit and independent, and tells you precisely which command to run for each mount
instead of guessing. In preview the calculus is different: there is no output directory to
get wrong, the mounted pages are rendered on request, and live mounting is what makes the
marketing site's `/docs/guide` and `/docs/internals` links work in one `preview` session,
so the live server takes on that convenience while the static build stays principled.

::: {.callout-tip}
Because a mounted page under `preview` is rendered as plain static output (no websocket, no
click-to-source), preview a mounted book *directly* (`preview docs/internals`) when you
want the full incremental live loop on it, and use the mount only to check that the
cross-project links resolve.
:::
